   141	## Gate results
   142	
   143	### Gate A — PASS
   144	
   145	- A1: `SPEC.target` loads and executes the literal translated body; the loop
   146	  theorem uses exact definitional aliases of that same body and continuation.
   147	- A2/A3: the bridge-free connection claims retain an arbitrary continuation.
   148	  The `isinstance` bridge was narrowed after a probe exposed the reference
   149	  dereference boundary; reference arguments now remain on the fixed path.
   150	- A4: evaluator twins have exhaustive, non-overlapping equations. The sole
   151	  opaque destructor is value-determined on every reachable observation domain.
   152	  No fresh result can select an unconstrained final outcome.
   153	- A5: the satisfiable empty-dictionary false-postcondition mutation failed and
   154	  displayed the actual false result.
   155	- Fixed and bridge-enabled ground executions were byte-identical.
   156	
   157	### Gate B — PASS
   158	
   159	The theorem covers arbitrary finite symbolic key sequences, not examples,
   160	fixed sizes, or bounded unrollings. The postcondition directly formalizes the
   161	prompt's nonempty/all-lower-or-all-upper contract, including rejection of
   162	non-string and uncased string keys. The no-reference premise removes values
   163	that cannot be well-formed keys in the supplied model's empty initial heap; it
   164	does not bound the material dictionary domain.
   165	
   166	The intended execution model for this benchmark is the supplied MPY semantics.
   167	Its string case predicates are ASCII-oriented. The Python implementation itself
   168	was additionally checked against CPython on Unicode examples, but universal
   169	equivalence between MPY's character model and CPython Unicode is not claimed.
